  <ferc:StatementAboutImportantChangesDuringTheQuarterYearTextBlock id="f-9" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;1. None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;2. None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;3. None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;4. None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;5. None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;6. None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;7. None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;8. None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;9. None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;10. None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;11 . None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;12. On August 1, 2024, Phillips 66 (&#8220;P66&#8221;) and The Williams Companies, Inc. (&#8220;WMB&#8221;) closed on a transaction that transferred DIGP and other interests to WMB. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;13. We continue to have recurring losses but have an agreement in place with our parent to fund future operating costs and obligations as they become due. See Note 1 in the Notes to Financial Statements.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:StatementAboutImportantChangesDuringTheQuarterYearTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfImportantDisclosuresRegardingTheFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="f-10" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;padding-left:27pt;text-indent:-9pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Note 1. SUMMARY OF SIGNIFICANT ACCOUNTING POLICIES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:1pt;padding-left:27pt;text-indent:-9pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;Organization and Basis of Presentation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt; &#8212; Dauphin Island Gathering Partners, or DIGP, us, we, our or the Partnership, was organized in 1993 for the purpose of constructing and operating gas gathering and pipeline &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;facilities in the Mobile Bay area of offshore Alabama. On August 1, 2024, Phillips 66 (&#8220;P66&#8221;) and The Williams Companies, Inc. (&#8220;WMB&#8221;) closed on a transaction that transferred DIGP and other interests to WMB. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;Post transaction, the Partnership is owned 81.10% by Williams Dauphin Island, LLC, or Williams Dauphin Island, and 18.90% by WFS Mobile Bay Processing, LLC, or WFS Mobile Bay Processing. Both Williams Dauphin Island and WFS Mobile Bay Processing are wholly owned subsidiaries of Williams Field Services Group, LLC (WFS), which is a wholly owned subsidiary of WMB. WFS operates the Partnership.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;WFS and its affiliates fund the operations and capital expenditures of the Partnership. We have no cash on the balance sheet as of December 31, 2025 and 2024. As of August 1, 2024, all of our cash management activity is performed by WFS. Prior to August 1, 2024, cash management activity was performed by wholly owned affiliates of P66. Net amounts due to or from the Partnership from or to affiliates are settled periodically by the transfer of such receivables and payables to WFS and are shown as Investments in and Advances to Associated and Subsidiary Companies in the Statement of Cash Flows. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Use of Estimate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;s &#8212; Conformity with FERC accounting rules and GAAP requires management to make estimates and assumptions that affect the amounts reported in the financial statements and footnotes. Although these estimates are based on management's best available knowledge of current and expected future events, actual results could be different from these estimates.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Property, Plant and Equipment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &#8212; Property, plant and equipment are recorded at original cost. The cost of maintenance and repairs, which are not significant improvements, are expensed when incurred. Depreciation is computed using the straight-line method over their estimated useful life of 3 to approximately 30 years.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Asset retirement obligations associated with tangible long-lived assets are recorded at fair value in the period in which they are incurred, if a reasonable estimate of fair value can be made and added to the carrying amount of the associated asset. This additional carrying amount is then depreciated over the life of the asset. The liability increases due to the passage of time based on the time value of money until the obligation is settled.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Regulatory Asset &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#8212; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Gross-Up on Allowance for Funds Used During Construction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;&#8212;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The Allowance for Funds Used During Construction, or AFUDC, represents the estimated costs, during the period of construction, of borrowed and equity funds used for construction purposes. Recognition of this allowance is considered appropriate because it constitutes an actual cost of construction. For its regulated activities, DIGP is permitted to earn a return on and recover AFUDC through its inclusion in rate base and the provision for depreciation. These amounts are capitalized and recorded in Property, Plant and Equipment. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Pipeline and Gathering Revenues &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;&#8212; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;For pipeline and gathering services, the Partnership receives fees from the producers to transport the natural gas from the wellhead to the processing plant. The Partnership recognizes pipeline and gathering revenues as gas volumes are transported through the pipeline and gathering facilities. When rate cases are pending final FERC approval, a portion of the revenue collected is subject to possible refund. Reserves are established where required for such cases; however, there were no outstanding cases and&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;no reserves recorded as of December 31, 2025 or December 31, 2024. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Revenues from transportation agreements are recognized based on contracted volumes transported in the period the services are provided. Our contracts generally have terms that extend beyond one year, and related revenues are recognized over time. The performance obligation for most of our contracts encompasses a series of distinct services performed on discrete daily quantities of natural gas for purposes of allocating variable consideration and recognizing revenue while the customer simultaneously receives and consumes the benefits of the transportation services provided. Revenue is recognized over time consistent with the transfer of services over time to the customer based on daily volumes delivered. Consideration is generally variable, and the transaction price cannot be determined at the inception of the contract, because the volume of natural gas for which the service is provided is only specified on a daily or monthly basis. The transaction price is determined at the time the service is provided as the uncertainty is resolved.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Quantities of natural gas over-delivered or under-delivered related to imbalance agreements with producers and pipelines are recorded monthly as receivables or payables using current market prices or the weighted-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;average prices of natural gas at the plant or system. These balances are settled with deliveries of natural gas, or with cash. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Income Taxes &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;&#8212; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The Partnership is not subject to income tax, but rather the taxable income or loss of the Partnership is reported on the respective income tax returns of its partners. The FERC requires that DIGP maintain its books of accounts and determine its rates as though it was a taxable corporate subsidiary of its partners. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On July 18, 2018, the Commission issued guidance in Docket No. PL17-1-001, regarding its revised policy on income tax allowance stating that the Commission &#8220;will no longer permit master limited partnerships to recover an income tax allowance in their cost&#8221;. The Commission state that if a pass-through pipeline eliminates its income tax allowance from its cost, the Commission anticipates that Accumulated Deferred Income Taxes (&#8220;ADIT&#8221;) will be removed from the cost.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Pursuant to the guidance from the July 18 Order, as of December 31,2018 DIGP has elected to eliminate previously accumulated ADIT balances in Accounts 190 (ADIT), 283 (ADIT-Other) of the Commission&#8217;s Uniform System of Accounts.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Going Concern&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;&#8212;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; The accompanying Financial Statements have been prepared on a going concern basis, which contemplates the continuity of operations, the realization of assets and the satisfaction of liabilities in the normal course of business.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;As of the date of these financial statements, we have generated recurring losses and negative cash flows from operations. We have not yet established an ongoing source of revenues that is sufficient to cover our future operating costs and obligations as they become due during the twelve months following the issuance of these financial statements. These conditions raise substantial doubt about our ability to continue as a going concern for twelve months following the issuance of these financial statements.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Management&#8217;s plans intended to mitigate these conditions include obtaining additional funding from WFS. WFS has committed to provide the Partnership with the necessary funding to enable the Partnership the necessary funding to meet its obligations as they become due as evidenced by a support letter received from WFS. As a result, the Partnership has concluded that management&#8217;s plans are probable of being achieved to alleviate substantial doubt about the Partnership&#8217;s ability to continue as a going concern. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;text-indent:22.5pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Certain operating cash flow classifications in 2024 were reclassified to conform to the 2025 operating cash flow presentation. The reclassification had no impact on operating cash flows.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;padding-left:27pt;text-indent:-9pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Note 2. BUSINESS CONCENTRATIONS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:1pt;padding-left:27pt;text-indent:-9pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;Four customers accounted for $2,103,934 or 93% of the Partnership&#8217;s revenues during the year ended December 31, 2025. Four customers accounted for $2,368,148, or 97% of the Partnership&#8217;s revenues during the year ended December 31, 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;padding-left:27pt;text-indent:-9pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Note 3. ASSET RETIREMENT OBLIGATIONS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:1pt;padding-left:27pt;text-indent:-9pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:1pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;DIGP&#8217;s asset retirement obligations relate primarily to pipeline and platform abandonments on the DIGP system. The asset retirement obligation is adjusted each quarter for any liabilities incurred or settled during the period, accretion expense and any revisions made to the estimated cash flows. Accretion expense related to asset retirement obligation is not recovered through rates nor is there any mechanism or account in which recovered funds are being placed (i.e. trust funds, insurance policies, surety bonds). The following table summarizes changes in the asset retirement obligation, included in Other Noncurrent Liabilities in the balance sheets:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt"&gt;&lt;table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:72.470%"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:62.349%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:16.764%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.621%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:16.766%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="9" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;December 31,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;2024&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Balance, beginning of period&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;12,878,979&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td style="padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;12,183,721&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Accretion expense&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;724,643&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;695,258&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Liabilities Incurred/Settled During the Period&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;(385,291)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#8212;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Balance, end of period&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-bottom:1pt solid #000;border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-bottom:1pt solid #000;border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;13,218,331&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-bottom:1pt solid #000;border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-bottom:1pt solid #000;border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td style="border-bottom:1pt solid #000;border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',s